    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0bf62390_a2fc6d90",
        "filename": "services/core/java/com/android/server/NetdUnsolicitedEventListenerService.java",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 80,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-09-28T09:57:16Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There is an expectation mismatch here. It\u0027s a bit difficult to explain, but I\u0027ll try to be as clear as I can.\n\nThere is an ALLOWED_CALLBACK_TYPES array which lists the allowable caller types. Each type is an integer. This code here behaves as if the literal values of the types do not matter.\nHowever, code in line 65 and 74 use the caller type as an index directly.\n\nWhat this means is : you code depends on an unstated assumption that the literal values of the types must be equal to their index in the array (otherwise, IndexOutOfBoundException), making the contents of the array redundant, and tripping readers of the isValidCallerType method who should think the type values are irrelevant.\n\nI think there are two ways to solve this. Either way is fine.\n\n1. Consider that the types have to be the index, sequential starting from 0.\n→ In this case, replace the ALLOWED_CALLBACK_TYPES array with a simple MAX_CALLBACK_TYPE integer, and simplify this method to \"return callerType \u003c\u003d MAX_CALLBACK_TYPE;\"\n2. Consider the types to be symbolic and their value to not matter.\n→ In this case you would rename this method to \"getCallerIndexFromType\" or something, have it return -1 if it\u0027s not found, and return the index if it is found. Then lines 65 and 74 would use the return value of this method instead.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 80,
        "startChar": 12,
        "endLine": 80,
        "endChar": 58
      },
      "revId": "9e23ce88d162c943c470f66c616aeb4973e7c308",
